    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Lifelong penicillamine or trientine, a low-copper diet, and sometimes oral zinc."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Penicillamine = the most used chelator but toxic (fever, rash, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, proteinuria; can occasionally worsen neuro Sx); give oral pyridoxine w it; cross-reactivity w penicillin allergy may occur. Trientine = the alt chelator."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Zinc acetate can reduce copper absorb: maintenance or intol of the chelators. Don't take penicillamine or trientine @ the same time as zinc (they bind it)."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
